@@ -0,0 +1,138 @@
+# Hegel property-based testing report: `percent-encoding`
+
+Scope: the `percent_encoding` workspace member only (the `url` crate is deliberately
+out of scope). Tests were added to the existing `#[cfg(test)]` modules in
+`percent_encoding/src/lib.rs` and `percent_encoding/src/ascii_set.rs`, using
+`hegeltest` 0.28.2 (added with `--ignore-rust-version`; the crate pins
+`rust-version = "1.51"`, hegeltest needs 1.86 — a maintainer decision if upstreamed).
+
+## Properties tested
+
+Eight properties. Generated `AsciiSet`s follow the configuration pattern: start
+from `CONTROLS`, add and remove drawn ASCII bytes (`Box::leak`ed because
+`percent_encode` demands `&'static AsciiSet`). Draws are bounded to
+`0x00..=0x7F` because `AsciiSet` is documented as ASCII-range only (see
+Suspected bugs).
+
+1. **`prop_decode_inverts_encode_when_set_has_percent`** (`src/lib.rs`) —
+   `percent_decode(percent_encode(bytes, set)) == bytes` for arbitrary byte
+   strings (up to 200+ bytes, including bytes in the set and non-ASCII) against
+   either the public `NON_ALPHANUMERIC` set or a generated set with `b'%'`
+   forced in.
+2. **`prop_decode_inverts_encode_for_percent_free_input`** (`src/lib.rs`) —
+   the same round-trip for *any* generated set (with or without `%`, so
+   including `CONTROLS`-like sets), for inputs containing no literal `%`.
+   Because these sets need not contain `%`, the encoded output here mixes
+   escapes with runs of raw unencoded bytes, so this also verifies the decoder
+   passes unencoded bytes through untouched while decoding the escapes around
+   them.
+3. **`prop_decode_matches_reference_decoder`** (`src/lib.rs`) — the decoder
+   agrees with a reference decoder written independently from its docs ("any
+   sequence of `%` followed by two hexadecimal digits is decoded"), on inputs
+   biased toward the malformed-escape boundary: lone `%`, truncated `%X`,
+   `%` + non-hex, and valid upper/lowercase escapes (uniform random bytes
+   almost never produce a valid `%XX` triple, so a dedicated composite
+   generator builds inputs from these fragments). Subsumes decode robustness /
+   no-panic on arbitrary garbage.
+4. **`prop_decode_views_agree`** (`src/lib.rs`) — all documented views of
+   `PercentDecode` agree with its iterator: `Cow::from` yields the same bytes
+   and borrows the input when nothing decodes (documented direction only),
+   `decode_utf8` agrees with `str::from_utf8` of the decoded bytes (both Ok and
+   Err cases), and `decode_utf8_lossy` agrees with `String::from_utf8_lossy`
+   (this exercises the `unsafe` owned-path optimization in
+   `decode_utf8_lossy`).
+5. **`prop_encode_matches_per_byte_oracle`** (`src/lib.rs`) — the encoder's
+   output equals a per-byte oracle built from the documented rule "non-ASCII
+   bytes and bytes in `ascii_set` are encoded" plus `percent_encode_byte`
+   (itself pinned by the pre-existing exhaustive unit test); also asserts the
+   output is pure ASCII. This targets the iterator's slice-chunking fast path.
+   Runs against generated sets and both public sets (`CONTROLS`,
+   `NON_ALPHANUMERIC`).
+6. **`prop_encode_views_agree`** (`src/lib.rs`) — `Display`, `collect::<String>()`,
+   and `Cow::from` views of `PercentEncode` agree, and the `Cow` borrows the
+   input when none of its bytes are encoded (documented direction only — see
+   Unsound properties).
+7. **`prop_utf8_encode_decode_roundtrip`** (`src/lib.rs`) —
+   `utf8_percent_encode` on arbitrary Unicode strings round-trips through
+   `percent_decode_str(...).decode_utf8()` for generated sets containing `%`,
+   and `decode_utf8_lossy` agrees (valid-UTF-8 consistency).
+8. **`prop_ascii_set_behaves_like_hashset_model`** (`src/ascii_set.rs`) —
+   stateful model test: `AsciiSet` under `add`/`remove`/`union`/`complement`
+   (randomly via the method or the documented `+`/`!` operator forms) agrees
+   with a `HashSet<u8>` model, checked after every step via `contains` over all
+   128 ASCII bytes and `should_percent_encode` over all 256 bytes.
+
+## Test results
+
+- Final suite: **26 unit tests passed (8 hegel properties + 18 pre-existing), 6 doc tests passed, 0 failed.** (`cargo test -p percent-encoding`)
+- Liveness verified twice: planting an off-by-one in the reference decoder
+  (`i + 2 <= input.len()`) made property 3 fail with the minimal shrunk
+  counterexample `[37, 0]` (`"%\0"`); inverting the model invariant made the
+  state machine fail on the initial invariant check. Both reverted; `.hegel/`
+  failure DB removed and added to `.gitignore`.
+- Exploratory 10x run: all 8 properties run at `test_cases = 1000` — all green;
+  reverted to the default count for the committed tests.
+- No failing tests are left in the tree; no bugs needed pinning.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+No behavioral bugs found in the encode/decode logic. Two observations worth
+recording (neither is asserted by a test, since both are outside documented
+promises):
+
+- **`AsciiSet::add`/`remove` panic with an unhelpful index-out-of-bounds on
+  non-ASCII bytes.** `AsciiSet::EMPTY.add(0x80)` panics at runtime with
+  `index out of bounds: the len is 4 but the index is 4`
+  (`src/ascii_set.rs:58`), and fails const-eval in const contexts. The type is
+  documented as "a set of characters or bytes in the ASCII range", so this is
+  out of the documented domain and generators were bounded accordingly — but a
+  `debug_assert!(byte.is_ascii())` or doc'd panic would make the failure mode
+  clearer. Flagging for the maintainer rather than fixing (per instructions).
+- **`Cow::from(PercentEncode)` can return `Cow::Borrowed` that does not borrow
+  the input**: an input whose encoding is a single escape (e.g. `[0x00]` under
+  `CONTROLS`) yields `Cow::Borrowed("%00")` borrowing the static escape table.
+  The docs only promise borrowing *"when none of its bytes are encoded"*, which
+  remains true — but code assuming `Borrowed` implies output == input would be
+  wrong. Property 6 asserts only the documented direction.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **Unconditional round-trip `decode(encode(x, set)) == x` for sets not
+  containing `%`.** Encoding with such a set leaves literal `%` unescaped, so
+  an input already containing `%41` encodes to a string that decodes to `A`.
+  The docs promise nothing here (`CONTROLS` and any user set built from it lack
+  `%`). Split into properties 1 (force `%` into the set) and 2 (`%`-free
+  inputs, arbitrary sets) instead.
+- **Encoding idempotence `encode(encode(x)) == encode(x)`.** Never promised,
+  and false for any set containing `%` or hex digits (e.g. `NON_ALPHANUMERIC`
+  contains `%`, so `%20` re-encodes to `%2520`). Not asserted (the
+  encode-set-boundary lesson).
+- **`Cow::Borrowed` ⟺ nothing encoded** for `PercentEncode` — the ⟸ direction
+  is documented and asserted; the ⟹ direction is false (see Suspected bugs).
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- **The `no_std` guidance needs a crate-root variant.** The Rust reference says
+  to add `extern crate std;` *inside the test module*, but this crate's root
+  does `extern crate std as _;` and `#[hegel::state_machine]` expands to paths
+  that resolve `std` at the crate root, failing with
+  ``E0433: cannot find `std` in the crate root ... in the list of imported
+  crates`` even with the module-level fix in place. The working fix was
+  `#[cfg(test)] extern crate std;` at the *crate root*. Worth documenting,
+  since `extern crate std as _;` is a common no_std forward-compat idiom.
+- **`#[hegel::composite]` returning references trips on `Box::leak`.**
+  `Box::leak` returns `&'static mut T`; inside the composite the macro's
+  return-type plumbing produced a confusing
+  `E0308 ... ComposedGenerator<&'static AsciiSet, _> / ComposedGenerator<&mut AsciiSet, ...>`
+  pointing at the attribute rather than the expression. An explicit
+  `let leaked: &'static AsciiSet = Box::leak(...); leaked` fixed it. A gotcha
+  note would save time.
+- **The `&'static config` pattern deserves a mention** in the "Generate
+  Configurations" section: APIs that take `&'static Config` (like
+  `percent_encode`) require leaking generated configurations; that this is fine
+  in tests wasn't obvious until reasoned through.
+- Positives: the imperative composite style made the malformed-escape input
+  generator trivial to write; the state machine API worked on the first
+  compile; shrinking produced the true minimal counterexample (`[37, 0]`)
+  during the liveness check; the "verify tests are live + one 10x run"
+  checklist caught nothing here but was cheap and confidence-building.
